            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Literary Works</strong><br>\nHis literary contributions include poems and writings on various subjects like religion, physics, and biology. Unfortunately, much of his work survives only in fragments. His poetry often dealt with religious and philosophical themes and reflected his comprehensive view of nature and humanity's place within it.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Theories on Nature</strong><br>\nBeyond the elements, Empedokles proposed the forces of Love (Philia) and Strife (Neikos) as the fundamental principles that caused the combination and separation of the elements. Love was the reason for union and creation in nature, while Strife was responsible for destruction and the division of elements.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Perception and Knowledge</strong><br>\nEmpedokles had distinct views on perception and knowledge. He believed our understanding of the world comes from the interaction between forces within us and external realities. For instance, he thought we could see because of the fire within our eyes meeting with external light.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
